00:07:25.254 --> 00:07:26.920
And that's a good
thing now, because now

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:28.586
with APIs and everything,
it sort of all

00:07:28.586 --> 00:07:29.430
fits into the model.

00:07:33.279 --> 00:07:35.070
I'm going to talk about
Manhattan's journey

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:38.250
in the decade of the
cloud, decade of mobile,

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.810
and the whole approach.

00:07:44.859 --> 00:07:46.900
I've been at Manhattan
College for like 20 years.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:48.569
And for the first
10, we basically

00:07:48.569 --> 00:07:49.860
started the web and everything.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.290
It seemed like not
much was happening.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:56.040
We were in the era of Web 1.0.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:58.440
You know, you had your web
server, people did stuff,

00:07:58.440 --> 00:07:59.190
whatever.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:04.800
And then about 10 years, Web
2.0 started to come into play,

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.350
and everything became
more intercollaborative.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:11.490
People started contributing
information and things.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:14.240
But that was also, I
think, when a lot of this--

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.900
everything as a service started.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.980
We started to see
a lot of software

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:19.690
as a service being introduced.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:24.291
The first one in that
case would be Gmail, OK?

00:08:24.291 --> 00:08:26.040
But most of those
things were all services

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.581
that are delivered
over the internet,

00:08:27.581 --> 00:08:29.790
rather than provided
locally or on-site.

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:32.400
So this thing has been growing
over the past 10 years,

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.809
or evolving.

00:08:33.809 --> 00:08:35.520
Today, especially
in higher ed, we're

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.630
seeing a lot of adopting of
best-of-breed applications.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:43.134
So rather than having
everything from one vendor,

00:08:43.134 --> 00:08:44.550
we're adopting a
bunch of products

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:46.080
from a lot of different vendors.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.030
And that presents a lot
of integration challenges.

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:52.473
So the role that we
play is quite different.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.590
So here's sort of a
simplified timeline

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:01.430
of Manhattan College and its
involvement with Google Cloud.

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:04.070
And again, I concentrate
on the last 10 years

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:07.130
because that's when
we started using

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:09.770
our first sofware as a service.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:11.810
And that was Gmail.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:13.767
That was pretty
revolutionary, at the time.

00:09:13.767 --> 00:09:15.350
We were probably one
of the first sets

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:18.085
of colleges to introduce that.

00:09:18.085 --> 00:09:22.070
That was part of the G
Suite, formerly called

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:24.080
Google Apps for Education.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.920
And so we started using a
lot of Google products--

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:31.160
Calendar, Docs, Forms, Sheets.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:35.570
In addition, we've always used,
for web, the Google Search,

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:38.580
Google Maps, Google Analytics.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.760
Another big thing that
we've more recently

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:45.680
adopted across the
campus was Google Drive.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:48.260
But rather than
the last 10 years,

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:52.310
I think the key defining
point for the college in terms

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:58.594
of moving to the Cloud was in
the time frame of 2014 to 2016.

00:09:58.594 --> 00:10:00.260
There were a lot of
things that happened

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.960
in the college that
just sort of gave us

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:06.140
opportunities to do this--

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:11.900
was basically what we call
Google Cloud, phase 1, when

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.180
Rob's group, the Enterprise
Architecture Group,

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:18.090
introduced a virtual
machine as a service.

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:21.740
So what that entailed was
migrating a lot of the web

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:25.790
applications over
to Google Cloud

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:27.704
and to the compute platform.

00:10:27.704 --> 00:10:29.120
And also at that
time, the college

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.840
was going through a rebranding
effort for their web sites.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:37.250
And part of that involved
us providing web servers.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:39.830
And rather than hosting
them in our data center,

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.090
they were now hosted
in the cloud--

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.250
on Google Cloud.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:46.595
And that was with also high
availability, load balancing

00:10:46.595 --> 00:10:47.360
and that.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:51.590
So that was a big
success for us.

00:10:51.590 --> 00:10:56.360
Eventually, that evolved more
into Google Cloud, phase 2,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:01.280
what we term the hybrid data
center where the database was

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:05.390
left in our data center, and
the other parts of the system

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:07.420
were out in the Google Cloud.

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.100
And that was facilitated
by the introduction

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:13.265
of site-to-site VPN between
the data center and the cloud.

00:11:13.265 --> 00:11:16.490
So now, we actually had
two parts of the system

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:19.590
running in different
places, but connected.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:23.180
And so we ran our first launch
of our enterprise research

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:30.020
planning system in the
cloud in around 2017.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:33.350
So our COO was very, very into
cloud and advancing cloud.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:36.710
And a cloud-first
strategy became

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:38.690
part of our annual goals.

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:48.630
Starting in 2016 and 2017,
we were told basically, no

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:49.370
more servers.

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:51.545
Everything that we get
now, any kind of service,

00:11:51.545 --> 00:11:53.300
is deployed in the Cloud.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:54.750
No new hardware.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:56.865
So that's sort of
been the mandate.

00:11:56.865 --> 00:11:59.150
It's been a little tough to
handle from the beginning,

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:00.970
but it's all OK now.

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:03.100
But everything has
been working well,

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:06.680
and we've been
pretty successful.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:08.138
Oops.

00:12:08.138 --> 00:12:09.110
What happened here?

00:12:17.372 --> 00:12:19.364
OK.

00:12:19.364 --> 00:12:21.780
So I'm going to talk a little
bit about the web technology

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.910
landscape prior
to 2014 and how we

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.950
used to develop our
applications and whatever.

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:31.345
We were pretty comfortable
and used a PHP-based LAMP

00:12:31.345 --> 00:12:37.150
stack, which was Linux
as the operating system;

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:40.590
Apache HTTP Server
as the web server;

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:43.080
MySQL as the
relational database,

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.890
and PHP as the
scripting language.

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:49.980
And that was used for most of
our custom development work,

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:52.860
but also to run
our web site, which

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:55.770
we were using open source
Drupal as our content management

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:56.760
system framework.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.100
We used to self-host that.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:03.900
And eventually, we
started moving that

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:08.970
to a platform-as-a-service
provider in 2014-2016.

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:11.810
So we got some experience
with the cloud from that.

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:15.160
That was a little tough.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:19.890
On the other end, we had
what we called My MC Portal.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:22.800
And I don't know-- if
some of you are younger,

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:25.710
when you were in college,
you might recall the portals

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:27.210
that your school used to have.

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.400
It was basically almost
like a monolithic system

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:33.900
of little windows into a bunch
of different applications,

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:37.320
a lot of it is connected
by single sign-on.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:41.580
That was pretty difficult
for us to pull off.

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:45.300
Now, first of all, we adopted a
open-source framework for that,

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.520
called uPortal.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.470
But that was a Java-based stack.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.610
It used Linux as the
operating system,

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:56.910
but it used Apache Tomcat
as the web server and Oracle

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:59.760
database, a relational
database, for the database.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.010
And then, it used Java.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:03.330
So we only have a small staff.

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:05.704
We had three people in it,
and only like one of them

00:14:05.704 --> 00:14:06.870
was a really good developer.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.510
So we learned a lot doing that.

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:11.290
We ran that for
about three years,

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.780
but it was pretty limited.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.300
And in the end, Marketing
and Communication

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:17.400
was looking for something
more of like people

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.160
can go in and have WYSIWYG
editor and enter content--

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:20.850
whatever.

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:24.210
So eventually, as part of
the web branding initiative

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:28.679
we got rid of that and moved
to something different.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:37.720
So again, a lot of things
happened in 2014 to 2016.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.330
And I just say, this was
sort of a defining period

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:43.600
for us, and especially
for the web.

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:45.422
Was it fate?

00:14:45.422 --> 00:14:46.560
Was it kismet?

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.130
Was it serendipity?

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:50.800
Everything just sort of
came together very nicely.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:53.230
Everything was happening
at the right time.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.780
The college was going
through a branding initiative

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:58.060
for the websites,
in which case, we

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:02.320
ended up with a vendor-hosted
content management system.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:07.890
There were also a
lot of campus master

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:11.320
plans planning taking effect.

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:12.850
The college had--
you recall that I

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:15.700
showed the new student commons.

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:19.090
With all that, they
finally licensed

00:15:19.090 --> 00:15:21.010
a room event-scheduling system.

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:24.160
So now, we had a lot
of calendars and stuff.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.890
And all this, everything
as a service was maturing.

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:30.040
And the best part for us and
for our adoption of Firebase

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:32.620
was we got summer interns.

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:36.820
The college had a Jasper
summer research program.

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:39.940
And prior to that, a lot
of other departments,

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.740
especially in Science
and Engineering,

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:45.330
were hiring students to
come up with a project they

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:46.370
wanted to work on.

00:15:46.370 --> 00:15:49.180
And they would fund it for
the summer, and whatever.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:52.176
So ITS took on--

00:15:52.176 --> 00:15:54.104
well, my group took
on two summer interns,

00:15:54.104 --> 00:15:56.270
and we had to come up with
a project for them to do.

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.660
So before I get into
the start of Firebase

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:08.820
and how we used that--

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:11.670
we're seeing a lot of shift
in technologies in development

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:12.900
at this time.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:15.150
Our content management system
and the publishing model

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:16.090
was different.

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:21.210
The websites were all developed
through a vendor SaaS solution.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.760
But what was happening
here is the web content

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.040
from that system was
basically published out

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:28.770
to web servers, sort of
like a static web site, OK?

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:34.030
It hooks into use feeds, and
widgets, and things like that.

00:16:34.030 --> 00:16:38.359
But a lot of the
stuff, even with PHP,

00:16:38.359 --> 00:16:39.900
was basically done
behind the scenes.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:41.990
And the website was
published out every night.

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.220
We published out every night.

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.290
So that gave us more
opportunity to start

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:49.290
working with data
integration via widgets

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:55.170
and feeds either in RSS XML
format, or more importantly,

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:58.450
the JSON format, which
we've sort of adopted.

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:04.770
And that's also part
of how Firebase works.

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:07.009
And the other end was like
the death of our portal.

00:17:07.009 --> 00:17:08.550
I don't know if that
was good or bad.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.425
In retrospect, it was
good, but we were still

00:17:10.425 --> 00:17:13.829
sort of a little sad
to see a lot of that go

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:17.250
and a lot of the work
we had done disappear.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:21.369
There were certain parts of
the portal that were useful.

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:23.369
And one of the things was--

00:17:23.369 --> 00:17:27.360
what they had was you were able
to search within a portlet.

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:30.350
And a portlet is basically
like on of those windows--

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:30.970
whatever.

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:34.590
But you basically would
enter some metadata

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:36.990
about what was in the portlet.

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:40.830
And lot of that was
used at the portal level

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.170
to help people find things.

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:45.480
Another thing that
the portal always had

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:47.730
was a lot of quick links.

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:50.700
You were starting to see
a lot of these everything

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.550
as a service products out there.

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:56.010
And people were using
the portal as a place

00:17:56.010 --> 00:18:00.550
where people would come and
find all those different links.

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:02.590
So it was basically
just a list of links.

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:05.040
But anyway, for the
searching, people

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.940
were starting to
see more of looking

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:09.680
at other kinds of solutions,
because the portal--

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:12.420
and pulling it off was a very
difficult concept, unless you

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:16.380
really licensed a version
from one of the big vendors.

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:19.560
But you were starting to
see more lightweight campus

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.590
portals where people
were starting to say,

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:25.110
these other products
do their thing well.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.510
They don't really want--
like for example, Google--

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:29.970
they don't really want you
pulling in your email threads

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:33.960
into a window in the portal,
and pulling your calendar stuff

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:35.510
into another window
of the portal,

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:37.994
and pulling in your files,
looking at another one.

00:18:37.994 --> 00:18:39.660
And basically, it had
its old interface,

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:42.450
and so roll with the flow.

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:44.850
[CHUCKLES]

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:48.330
But those were some of the
good features of the portal

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:52.290
that people like to keep.

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:55.020
And again, it was a big
learning experience for us.

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:56.490
It was open source product.

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.890
We learned a lot
about developing code

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:01.720
and contributing code--

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:02.220
whatever.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:09.910
So a big thing for
the new and what

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:12.400
they called an
intranet website was

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:15.820
a way of aggregating all these
campus tasks and services.

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:17.740
And to do that--

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:20.500
and this is also happening in
conjunction at the same time

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:22.990
that we started
developing a student app--

00:19:22.990 --> 00:19:28.300
was a way to aggregate these
someplace so that people

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:29.830
could find the information.

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:31.570
And the approach
we basically took

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:37.020
was defining things like, pay
my bill, things like that.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:39.160
And it might be
embedded in some system,

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:43.540
but people would search for
"pay my bill", or "tuition",

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:47.680
or whatever, or "find
the final exam schedule".

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.900
And they would find it
through this interface.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:52.089
And for this, we used a
search as a service product

00:19:52.089 --> 00:19:52.630
from Algolia.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:20:01.420
So now we get into developing
the Glance MC Student App.

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:05.288
And it was named like
that because of some

00:20:05.288 --> 00:20:07.246
of the conditionals we
put on this development.

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:14.105
So again, our native
mobile app was a summer

00:20:14.105 --> 00:20:18.440
of 2015 student project.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.852
The students had to
come up with a project.

00:20:20.852 --> 00:20:21.935
I came back from vacation.

00:20:21.935 --> 00:20:23.393
It's like, here's
your two student.

00:20:23.393 --> 00:20:25.250
It's like, they need a project.

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:28.631
So at that time, everybody
wanted to develop apps.

00:20:28.631 --> 00:20:31.130
Well, they came up with this--
there's this word cloud here.

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:33.080
And things that
were hot at the time

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:40.220
were, mobile, discovery, portal,
lightweight, digital, search,

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:42.800
native, personal assistant.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:46.150
The big thing was
the campus ID card,

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:47.460
what we call our Jasper Card.

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:50.920
Manhattan College-- I didn't
mention this-- are the Jaspers.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:56.990
So I'll keep using the ID
card and Jasper card together.

00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:02.750
But students basically
carry their phones

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:03.800
around all the time.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:08.862
A lot of times, they were
forgetting their paper ID card.

00:21:08.862 --> 00:21:10.820
With all the new buildings
that were happening,

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:14.750
they were starting to use
these optical scanners to allow

00:21:14.750 --> 00:21:20.210
entry into the dorms,
and to the athletic gym,

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:22.130
and other places like that.

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:23.870
So the students kept
saying, oh, it'd

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:27.170
cool if we had our ID
card on our phones.

00:21:27.170 --> 00:21:34.080
So all that was part of the
visioning for this student app.

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:35.090
So then we had a choice.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:40.580
OK, what do we use for the
stack to develop this app?

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:44.390
At the time, you reallly
had Apple, you had Android,

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:48.230
or we could develop
a cross-platform app.

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:51.110
There were, at the time, a
predominance of Apple devices

00:21:51.110 --> 00:21:52.230
on our campus.

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:55.370
Students might have had a phone,
so the ecosystem from Apple.

00:21:55.370 --> 00:21:57.410
It could have been
like the laptop,

00:21:57.410 --> 00:22:01.400
they had a phone, they
had an iPad, or whatever.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.981
And the students wanted
to develop apps for Apple

00:22:03.981 --> 00:22:05.480
and felt that
development was easier

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:08.480
there because they only had
a couple of different sizes.

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:11.030
You had a couple of
different devices,

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:13.160
and they were all the same.

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:15.800
So we looked at Android, and you
had a lot of different phones

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:17.510
you had to worry about.

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:19.910
Plus, the students that we
had were also just going

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:21.180
into their junior year.

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:23.689
They really hadn't had any--

00:22:23.689 --> 00:22:25.730
the college really didn't
have a computer science

00:22:25.730 --> 00:22:27.400
program at the time.

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:29.930
The students didn't have any
kind of training or anything,

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:35.270
but they just felt comfortable
developing for Apple.

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:39.470
And I guess Swift had
just come out-- whatever.

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:42.840
For Android, they
had Android Studio--

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:43.850
whatever.

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:46.330
And it wasn't that
well-documented at the time,

00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:49.670
or the students felt.
So the other choice

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:54.684
was a cross-platform app,
and they really couldn't

00:22:54.684 --> 00:22:55.850
get their hands around that.

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:59.040
And the whole idea of
thinking about that was tough.

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:02.440
So who do you think won?

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:09.780
First app was an iOS app, under
the condition that the next one

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:11.880
would be Android.

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:14.850
So we had these two
students working all summer.

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:20.250
They were funded as a Jasper
Research Scholar at the time.

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:23.550
We've employed them
during the school years,

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:25.650
using campus employment
work-study funds.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:30.370
And we also allowed
them to learn.

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:31.611
We provide a lot of training.

00:23:31.611 --> 00:23:33.360
There's a lot of
self-study, self-learning

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:35.230
that goes on in my group.

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:37.120
We have a lot of
resources for that.

00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:41.280
But anyway, they ended
up with conceiving

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:45.900
a lightweight mobile app.

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:47.850
And they wanted
something where they can

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:49.741
access information on-the-go.

00:23:49.741 --> 00:23:51.990
One of the conditions from
Marketing and Communication

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:56.235
was that it complemented the new
websites that were coming out.

00:23:56.235 --> 00:23:59.640
And also, the college did
not have its own mobile app

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:01.590
and didn't see a need for one.

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.390
The websites were
all responsive,

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:06.630
and they felt that
was good enough.

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:10.830
But part of our justification
for using a native mobile app

00:24:10.830 --> 00:24:14.040
was that we would use the
features of the device,

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:16.260
like maps and
locations, and also

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:18.930
that we'd have a digital
campus ID card for the use

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:20.240
with optical scanners, OK?

00:24:20.240 --> 00:24:22.680
And so the way we
worked it was this

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:24.600
was kept as a student
developer product.

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:26.174
My group had oversight.

00:24:26.174 --> 00:24:28.590
And it sort of evolved a lot
into some healthy competition

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:30.540
between the two groups
where we learned a lot

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:31.510
from what they were doing.

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:33.660
We also sort of vet what
they're doing to make sure

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:34.990
they're on the right track.

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:37.810
But there's a lot
of competition.

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:41.270
So now, I'm going to get into
developing the Glance Student

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:44.970
App and our app basics
or data modeling.

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:46.440
So the first thing
they had to do

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:48.190
to come up with what
they wanted to put in

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:49.452
is collect all the data.

00:24:49.452 --> 00:24:51.910
And we needed to have someplace
where they could collect it

00:24:51.910 --> 00:24:53.590
and where we could see it.

00:24:53.590 --> 00:25:00.040
So the database code for that
was basically a Google Sheet.

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:02.530
So they collected all the
information in a Google Sheet.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:05.440
A lot of it was like
Maps information--

00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:10.599
coordinates, building codes,
the addresses, things like that.

00:25:10.599 --> 00:25:12.640
This is a little bit more
sophisticated than what

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:13.630
they had at the point.

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:15.790
But a lot of the stuff
we had from the portal

00:25:15.790 --> 00:25:19.090
were feeds-- you know, daily
announcements, news, twitters,

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:21.430
features, events,
events calendars--

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:23.950
lots of events calendars
with the new system

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:25.430
they had just put in place.

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:27.462
And so they collected it
all in a Google Sheet,

00:25:27.462 --> 00:25:29.265
and then they used
this data when they

00:25:29.265 --> 00:25:30.390
started developing the app.

00:25:32.747 --> 00:25:34.580
So when the app was
released, they basically

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:37.550
wrote all the stuff
into the code.

00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:41.730
We released it a year later--
a year and three months later,

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:44.990
called Glance MC, because
Glance meaning that we really

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:48.500
didn't want them to incorporate
things in there that

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:50.882
would get replaced by an app
that came out in the future.

00:25:50.882 --> 00:25:53.090
For example, if our ERP
vendor came up with something

00:25:53.090 --> 00:25:54.920
and this college
adopted it, we didn't

00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:56.720
want to have to throw
away what we did.

00:25:56.720 --> 00:26:00.350
So they were supposed to keep
more to top-level type stuff

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:01.100
here.

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:02.605
So we released it.

00:26:02.605 --> 00:26:06.200
And at the same time,
we released Jasper Pay

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:09.500
at the college, which allowed
people to pay for their meals

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:12.960
and stuff using their
digital ID card.

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:14.600
And that's become a big hit.

00:26:14.600 --> 00:26:16.485
We also employed in
there-- this will

00:26:16.485 --> 00:26:18.110
be called the At A
Glance search, which

00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:21.110
was part of the
aggregated campus services

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:23.810
that we show that we use
on our intranet portal.

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:27.860
And we have a employee directory
and all those services in it.

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:29.870
And we also incorporated
a bunch of RSS

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:32.800
and XML feeds and
a campus newspaper.

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:36.639
With time, we've added in
geolocation maps integration,

00:26:36.639 --> 00:26:38.930
so that someone can see, if
they looked up an employee,

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:41.221
it would tell them-- if they
clicked the location icon,

00:26:41.221 --> 00:26:43.250
it would show their
location on a map

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:47.510
and where they were relative to
the place where the person was

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:48.024
located.

00:26:48.024 --> 00:26:49.190
Same thing for the calendar.

00:26:49.190 --> 00:26:50.480
If the event was
happening someplace,

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:51.530
you could see where it was.

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:52.730
Part of the problem
with all that,

00:26:52.730 --> 00:26:54.480
too, is we couldn't
really use directions,

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:57.200
because our campus is
not mapped out by Google.

00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:58.815
You notice there's
a ring road that

00:26:58.815 --> 00:27:00.440
runs around the campus
and upper campus

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:02.670
with all these buildings
contained within.

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:08.140
So here comes the
turning point now.

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:10.210
We've developed this
release, this app for iOS.

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:13.480
And now, we had to
worry about Android.

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:17.620
So we wanted to match the
features from the iOS app

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:19.270
to the Android one.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:22.652
And then for further
development of the iOS app,

00:27:22.652 --> 00:27:24.110
there's a lot of
things they wanted

00:27:24.110 --> 00:27:25.330
incorporate, like favorites.

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:27.462
Once they found things
that they liked to search,

00:27:27.462 --> 00:27:29.920
they woud like to pin them so
that they have access to them

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:34.030
all the time, their
own favorites.

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:36.790
They wanted to have an
in-app campus ID card.

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:39.550
So the way we
deployed the campus ID

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:41.320
card was through Apple Wallet.

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:45.220
And we actually are
using a PKPASS file

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:48.220
to do that, which
my group had set up.

00:27:48.220 --> 00:27:51.220
Eventually, when we released
this app for Android,

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:54.661
they coupled it with a--

00:27:54.661 --> 00:27:57.850
I think it was called a--

00:27:57.850 --> 00:27:59.939
Wallet passed this
thing from Android

00:27:59.939 --> 00:28:01.480
so they could get
the campus ID card.

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:04.300
But if we had an
in-app Jasper card,

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:08.434
then people didn't need
the other application.

00:28:08.434 --> 00:28:09.850
Eventually, we
want to have access

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:13.239
to authorized data
via authentication.

00:28:13.239 --> 00:28:15.780
Like people wanted to see their
course schedule on the phone.

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:18.645
We still had a lot of commuters
or people that live off campus.

00:28:18.645 --> 00:28:20.020
And a lot of times
if they wanted

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:21.850
to go to our student
information system,

00:28:21.850 --> 00:28:24.839
they have to go through
a lot of different steps

00:28:24.839 --> 00:28:25.880
to get to their schedule.

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:29.110
And it was a form that they
couldn't read on the phone, OK?

00:28:29.110 --> 00:28:30.760
So part of the problem
with all of this

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:34.255
now was the app data is
actually on the device.

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:38.996
It was really difficult
to get something

00:28:38.996 --> 00:28:40.245
released into the Apple Store.

00:28:40.245 --> 00:28:41.704
There's a long process.

00:28:41.704 --> 00:28:43.870
And plus, anytime we wanted
to change things-- there

00:28:43.870 --> 00:28:45.310
were things that
were always coming.

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:47.230
And somebody would say,
can you incorporate this?

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:49.230
You know, we changed
calendars, we changed fees,

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:51.100
we do whatever.

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:54.604
And so that was an issue.

00:28:54.604 --> 00:28:56.770
And so we sort of saw the
need for a mobile back end

00:28:56.770 --> 00:28:58.730
as a service for this.

00:28:58.730 --> 00:29:01.360
And part of the whole
thing was this--

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:03.500
app development
takes a lot of time.

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:05.260
So part of our
Agile methodology--

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:07.864
let's rethink it and see
what we need to do next.

00:29:07.864 --> 00:29:09.280
And oh, the other
choice was also,

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:14.260
should we switch to
something like React Native

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:15.850
to develop a cross-platform app.

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:26.470
So now developing the app,
take two-- enter Firebase.

00:29:26.470 --> 00:29:29.140
So the concept that
students had at the time

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:31.900
was to use Parse as
a mobile backend.

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:36.220
And then, that was
announced that they

00:29:36.220 --> 00:29:37.660
were getting rid of that.

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:41.380
But a good thing was
Firebase was acquired

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:44.500
by Google in October of 2014.

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:46.540
So that was the
thing we looked into.

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:49.300
And the first step was to
remove the data from the app

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.572
so that we could share
it across app versions.

00:29:51.572 --> 00:29:53.530
So here's where we have
the model for Firebase.

00:29:53.530 --> 00:29:58.870
And at the time, there was
Firebase Realtime Database

00:29:58.870 --> 00:30:01.990
where you could access the
same database from an iOS

00:30:01.990 --> 00:30:05.110
or an Android device
and also via the web.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:07.820
And the data gets
synced in real time.

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:13.490
So how do you think we
propagated our Firebase

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:14.700
Realtime Database?

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:19.970
We go back to our
original Google Sheet, OK?

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:22.910
Somebody wrote a
little F-Script to take

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:29.980
the data from the spreadsheet
and upload it to Firebase.

00:30:29.980 --> 00:30:32.030
OK, and that's an
example of just sending

00:30:32.030 --> 00:30:34.935
the data from the little
excerpt on the left of code

00:30:34.935 --> 00:30:36.560
is just what it takes
to send something

00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:37.726
from one Sheet to the other.

00:30:37.726 --> 00:30:39.800
If somebody goes in now
and changes something,

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:41.250
they added a new thing.

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:42.150
It's real-time.

00:30:42.150 --> 00:30:46.237
It shows up in the app, and
it syncs over to Firebase.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:53.740
And so this is a little
screenshot of our Firebase

00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:56.870
Realtime Database.

00:30:56.870 --> 00:31:01.570
The Firebase Realtime
Database is a NoSQL database,

00:31:01.570 --> 00:31:04.220
which means it's not
structured in tables,

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:07.950
like relational databases are.

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:10.970
But the big thing now is all
the app data is in the cloud.

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:12.950
And this is a more
recent version,

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:16.370
but you'll see here some of the
things that we have in here--

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:17.540
campus alerts.

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:19.700
We actually have
tutorials there that

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:23.690
refer to pictures out
in the cloud someplace.

00:31:23.690 --> 00:31:29.390
We have our building calendars,
feeds, tutorials, and then

00:31:29.390 --> 00:31:32.370
eventually users, because
we incorporated favorites

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:32.870
going on.

00:31:32.870 --> 00:31:35.266
So it gets a little
more complicated.

00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:42.890
And it was actually pretty
easy to set all this up.

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:47.610
So that was a big reason
to use Firebase too.

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:57.120
So after that, we started using
more of the Firebase ecosystem.

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:59.850
The keyword here was
Firebase Authentication

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:03.610
where people can sign in
by their Manhattan College

00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:07.410
or Google email
address and password.

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:11.940
That facilitated the development
of an in-app campus ID card

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:14.430
and allowed people to
pin their favorites.

00:32:14.430 --> 00:32:21.460
And I've showed example
of the ID card here.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:23.490
It basically just has
a barcode, whatever.

00:32:23.490 --> 00:32:25.740
And also, there's a place
where people could add funds

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:30.055
to their account.

00:32:30.055 --> 00:32:31.680
It actually has a
picture and all that.

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:34.650
So people were using this
now to swipe into dorms.

00:32:34.650 --> 00:32:37.030
When they swiped in, the
person on the other end

00:32:37.030 --> 00:32:38.730
would see a picture
of the student

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:44.832
from our database in the
college to identify the person.

00:32:44.832 --> 00:32:46.290
And also, they were
using it to pay

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:49.090
for their meals
and other things.

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:51.860
It actually gives them
their balance and whatever.

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:55.279
It's pretty immediate
when it gets updated.

00:32:55.279 --> 00:32:56.820
And also, here's
the thing where they

00:32:56.820 --> 00:33:03.030
can favorite their employees
and also their favorite services

00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:04.440
from the Algolia search.

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:12.470
Another part of the Firebase
ecosystem we started adopting

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:13.445
was Cloud Firestore.

00:33:13.445 --> 00:33:16.040
And that was in beta.

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:20.216
And that was a little
different model

00:33:20.216 --> 00:33:21.340
for how the data is stored.

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:23.548
Part of the problem with
using the real-time database

00:33:23.548 --> 00:33:26.720
was it only allowed you to
use string type of data.

00:33:26.720 --> 00:33:29.210
And if you wanted
something more structured,

00:33:29.210 --> 00:33:30.800
the programmers
had to write a lot

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:33.840
to be able to work with that.

00:33:33.840 --> 00:33:36.094
So Cloud Firestore, again,
is a NoSQL database,

00:33:36.094 --> 00:33:37.510
but it's a lot
more sophisticated.

00:33:37.510 --> 00:33:43.340
And it allows for
more types of data.

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:46.580
And the use case for
that was a quick view

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:49.580
of student's semester schedule
with an integrated map

00:33:49.580 --> 00:33:51.630
and instructor directory.

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:56.540
And here, we have a screenshot
where they released my courses

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:57.440
for the spring.

00:33:57.440 --> 00:34:03.590
And it actually shows a
student's schedule over here.

00:34:03.590 --> 00:34:08.989
They haven't yet linked it to
the faculty member's profile

00:34:08.989 --> 00:34:10.653
and also the building directory.

00:34:14.360 --> 00:34:17.360
So the Firebase
Document Collection

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:20.780
for the Cloud
Firestore basically

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:23.760
consists of a collection
with documents.

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:26.360
So the way they
have it set up is

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:29.389
you have a collection
of students.

00:34:29.389 --> 00:34:33.440
So each one of these
would be a student.

00:34:33.440 --> 00:34:37.031
A student would have a bunch of
information organized by term.

00:34:37.031 --> 00:34:38.989
First of all, we started
doing it for one term,

00:34:38.989 --> 00:34:42.620
and that's how we
initially released it.

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:44.699
Students can register
for the next semester

00:34:44.699 --> 00:34:47.480
at a certain time, and they
like to see their schedule

00:34:47.480 --> 00:34:48.409
on their phones.

00:34:48.409 --> 00:34:51.230
It was hard to just display
the current semester.

00:34:51.230 --> 00:34:54.050
They really needed a range
of semesters and all that.

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:56.330
So it's organized by term.

00:34:56.330 --> 00:35:03.410
And each term, a student
would have a bunch of classes.

00:35:03.410 --> 00:35:05.300
So that's how that's structured.

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:07.070
A lot went into the
thinking as to how

00:35:07.070 --> 00:35:09.265
they wanted to organize this.

00:35:09.265 --> 00:35:10.640
And the good part
was, because we

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:12.860
have an iOS developer
and an Apple developer,

00:35:12.860 --> 00:35:15.590
the two of them sort of vet
things out amongst themselves

00:35:15.590 --> 00:35:18.290
and eventually choose the
right way to approach it.

00:35:20.978 --> 00:35:22.974
And this sort of shows--
it's hard to show,

00:35:22.974 --> 00:35:24.640
I was going to show
this on a timeline--

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:27.470
but how the app was
developed over time.

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:29.140
We started out for iOS.

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:31.960
And eventually we started
with the Android one

00:35:31.960 --> 00:35:34.160
and sort of played a
little bit of catch-up.

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:38.142
But because of how
we adopted Firebase--

00:35:38.142 --> 00:35:39.850
and we didn't have it
ata the beginning--

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:41.800
the people doing the iOS
development had to go

00:35:41.800 --> 00:35:43.780
and refactor a lot of the
code and start over again,

00:35:43.780 --> 00:35:45.071
because they ripped stuff out--

00:35:45.071 --> 00:35:46.190
whatever.

00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:47.800
The Android person
started out fresh

00:35:47.800 --> 00:35:51.955
with Firebase and
all the products.

00:35:51.955 --> 00:35:54.165
And it has been a very
quick evolution for that.

00:35:54.165 --> 00:35:56.290
And actually, he's probably
ahead of the iOS person

00:35:56.290 --> 00:35:57.850
right now.

00:35:57.850 --> 00:36:04.660
So we used the Realtime
Database for basic app data.

00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:08.884
We used Authentication
for the Jasper card

00:36:08.884 --> 00:36:11.050
and also for favorites, and
then the Cloud Firestore

00:36:11.050 --> 00:36:13.660
for the core schedule.

00:36:13.660 --> 00:36:16.670
There's a bunch of other
ecosystem tools in Firebase.

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:19.720
For stability, we have
Crashalytics, crash reporting.

00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:22.810
And they also have Firebase
Test Lab, which we used.

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:24.970
And it also has
built-in analytics,

00:36:24.970 --> 00:36:27.436
Google Analytics for Firebase.

00:36:27.436 --> 00:36:28.810
Rather than show
a demo, I'm just

00:36:28.810 --> 00:36:32.470
going to do a quickie overview,
a promotional video of the app.

00:36:32.470 --> 00:36:34.430
It's sort of quick.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:38.740
But we've introduced this
for orientation back in June.

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:43.444
So it gets students into using
the app and their ID card.

00:36:43.444 --> 00:36:44.110
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:36:44.110 --> 00:36:46.102
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:37:48.352 --> 00:37:49.846
[END PLAYBACK]

00:37:53.662 --> 00:37:54.328
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:37:54.328 --> 00:37:55.822
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:37:55.822 --> 00:37:57.316
[END PLAYBACK]

00:38:01.370 --> 00:38:03.065
OK so a lot of
the sort of effect

00:38:03.065 --> 00:38:05.697
of where we're going with web
and web applications today,

00:38:05.697 --> 00:38:08.030
we sort of see a lot of this
thing as an app convergence

00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:11.600
journey where we'd like to get
these two native apps into one

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:12.200
in some way.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:15.230
And we're looking at maybe
the Firebase Cloud Functions

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:17.430
into helping us do that.

00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:21.670
We've also started to
develop a lot of web apps.

00:38:21.670 --> 00:38:23.579
And it's a lot quicker
to develop that way.

00:38:23.579 --> 00:38:25.370
And we've been
incorporating them into here

00:38:25.370 --> 00:38:27.870
by just a set of links.

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:29.720
Our web application
development stack

00:38:29.720 --> 00:38:33.350
has changed quite a bit
as a result of mostly

00:38:33.350 --> 00:38:34.550
how we were using Firebase.

00:38:34.550 --> 00:38:36.380
And it's a lot different today.

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:39.740
We're using a lot of APIs,
different kinds of libraries

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:42.890
and stuff, and even being
able to run on no JS

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:44.830
on the Google App Stack.

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:51.860
[INAUDIBLE] some of
this [INAUDIBLE]..

00:38:51.860 --> 00:38:53.720
We've also taken some
of these concepts

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:58.160
and have experimented with
a bunch of Google Assistant

00:38:58.160 --> 00:38:59.520
actions.

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:04.670
The most recent one is
Talk to Manhattan College,

00:39:04.670 --> 00:39:06.530
and it's a way to
extend the assistant

00:39:06.530 --> 00:39:10.700
and use the dialect flow, a
conversational action piece,

00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:12.770
to leverage the data from
Firebase and the app.

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:19.530
I'm running out of time here, so
I won't show this little demo.

00:39:22.709 --> 00:39:25.250
And again, we're using Analytics
via BigQuery and Data Studio

00:39:25.250 --> 00:39:28.060
to look at some of the
information from the app.

00:39:30.710 --> 00:39:33.070
So this is a segueway into
actually learning more about

00:39:33.070 --> 00:39:37.510
how we would use Cloud SQL
to analyze and bring together

00:39:37.510 --> 00:39:40.100
other data sets from the
college at a higher level.

00:39:43.730 --> 00:39:46.120
So in conclusion,
the Firebase platform

00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:49.360
has been a great entry point for
integrating with other Google

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:50.080
Cloud tools.

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:53.380
And they've been a valuable
partner in our continual cloud

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:54.560
journey.

00:39:54.560 --> 00:39:56.500
The best part was that
it was a way for us

00:39:56.500 --> 00:40:00.924
all to learn how to develop
with the newest tools.

00:40:00.924 --> 00:40:03.220
OK, thank you.

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:03.820
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:03.820 --> 00:40:07.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

